      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's just that \"proof-of-concept\" can sometimes be a red flag for reviewers at this specific symposium—they tend to look for high-powered studies. I'm worried that if we lean too hard on the \"feasibility\" angle, they'll just reject it on the grounds that the data isn't conclusive enough for their standards. \n\nIs there any way we can present the $n=12$ data so it doesn't look like a \"pilot\" or a \"smaller study,\" but rather like a complete and sufficient dataset for the specific phenomena we're looking at? I want to avoid the word \"pilot\" if possible.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
